// **Wrapping arithmetic near the top of the range, at compile time** (ADR-0116 §2). A regression test for a
// comptime miscompile the `Map` module surfaced and this fixes.
//
// The wrapping operators (`+%`, `-%`, `*%`) exist because plain arithmetic traps (ADR-0002): they are the
// opt-out that discards the bits that overflow. But the compiler's own constant evaluator decoded its operands
// to `i128` and computed `a *% b` as `wrap(a * b)` — and for two `u64` operands near the top of the range,
// `a * b` is about 2^128, which **overflows `i128` itself** and panicked before `wrap` could take the low bits.
//
// Native code has no `i128` intermediary — it multiplies in a 64-bit register and wraps — so it was correct
// while the VM panicked. That is an engine divergence, and it is exactly what the differential harness exists to
// catch: the `Map` test exited on a VM panic against a correct native result. The fix does the wrapping
// arithmetic on the truncated `u64` values, which is what `*%` always promised.
//
// This file multiplies two large `u64`s at **compile time** (through `#run`), the smallest reproduction —
// independent of the hash table that surfaced it. It also does the same at run time, so both engines are
// checked, and it checks `+%` and `-%` at the boundary too, since all three shared the bug.
//
// The exit code is **7**, three groups of one bit.
#import "Basic";

/// A wrapping multiply of two values near `u64`'s maximum, evaluated at compile time. Before the fix this
/// panicked the constant evaluator; the value itself is unimportant, only that it computes.
folded_mul :: () -> u64 {
    a: u64 = 11400714819323198485;
    return a *% a;
}

COMPUTED :: #run folded_mul();

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // The comptime value exists — the constant evaluator did not panic building it.
    c := COMPUTED;
    if c > 0 {
        n = n + 1;
    }

    // The same multiply at run time, in both engines.
    x: u64 = 11400714819323198485;
    y := x *% x;
    if y == c {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // `+%` and `-%` at the boundary shared the bug: adding past the top wraps to a small value, and subtracting
    // below zero wraps to a large one.
    hi: u64 = 18000000000000000000;
    if hi +% hi < hi {
        if 0 -% 1 > hi {
            n = n + 4;
        }
    }

    exit(n);
}
